INT. TINY APARTMENT - KITCHEN - NIGHT

A narrow galley kitchen. Mismatched mugs. A window over the sink opens onto a brick wall and one square of sky.

JOANNA CRUZ-DELACROIX (30s) stands at the counter in an oversized cardigan. A phone sits face-down beside a bowl of cold noodles. She stirs them, doesn't eat. She picks up the phone, flips it, sets it down.

Her thumb hovers over the screen. She takes a breath. Presses.

An old voice message begins to play through the speaker, tinny and warm. It is her own voice, years younger.

JOANNA (V.O.)
(recording)
Hi, it's me. If you're hearing this, I'm probably already on the train.

She mouths the next words along with the recording.

JOANNA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording)
Don't wait up. Save me some of that terrible soup.

She stops the playback. Her free hand rises to her collarbone, where a thin chain holds a small silver ring.

She walks to the window. Opens it, and cold air rushes in. She breathes it deeply.

She returns to the counter. Picks up the bowl. Tips the cold noodles into the sink, rinses the bowl, sets it on the rack.

She fills the kettle. Lights the stove. Two mugs sit on the shelf. She takes down one. Then, after a moment, the second.

She sets them side by side. The kettle begins to tick. She takes the ring off its chain, holds it in her palm, and slips it onto her finger.

JOANNA (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(recording, faint)
Save me some.

She smiles, finally, and the kettle starts to sing.
